{
  // The renderer is a second program, not a second source of truth: it compiles
  // the same src/ tree for Chromium — DOM types, ESNext modules, no Node — while
  // tsconfig.json compiles it for the main process. It emits its own JavaScript
  // into build/renderer, so scripts/build.mjs runs it after the static assets
  // are copied and before rustc writes the kernel into the same directory.
  //
  // rootDir is "src" rather than "src/renderer" because the renderer's type-only
  // imports of src/shared put those files in this program, and TypeScript
  // refuses a rootDir that does not contain every source file it may emit —
  // there is no flag that keeps a file in the program but out of the emit.
  // "src" is what makes the renderer's own output land flat in build/renderer,
  // which is the layout index.html and the gw://app protocol expect; the
  // alternative, an inferred common source directory, would bury it under
  // build/renderer/renderer and put a second copy of the shared contracts
  // beside it.
  //
  // The cost, stated rather than hidden: this project also emits the shared
  // modules the renderer references into build/shared, which tsconfig.json owns
  // and emits with sourcemaps. Whichever compiler runs last wins, so
  // scripts/build.mjs runs this one first and the main program second, and
  // tests/unit/the-companion-kernel-is-compiled-once-per-build.test.ts asserts
  // that order. Before it did, this project ran last and left build/shared/*.js
  // with no sourceMappingURL beside .js.map files nothing referenced.
  //
  // TypeScript's only way to drop the duplicate outright is a project reference
  // to tsconfig.json, which requires "composite": true there. Measured rather
  // than assumed: it works, and it emits a clean build/renderer with no shared
  // copy at all. It costs two things. `composite` is contagious — it applies to
  // every program built from tsconfig.json's options, including the one
  // tests/unit/diagnostic-schema-rejects-free-text.test.ts constructs to prove
  // the closed schema rejects free text, which then fails on "Composite
  // projects may not disable declaration emit" and on composite's demand that a
  // program list all of its files. And it forces `tsc -b`, whose `--noEmit` is
  // rejected (TS6310), so `pnpm typecheck` could only check a build by
  // producing one. Worth revisiting; not worth either price for a sourcemap.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": [],
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    // `pnpm typecheck` overrides this back to --noEmit. Checking is not
    // building: without the override this project writes build/renderer and
    // build/shared, so a bare `pnpm typecheck` after a build replaced the main
    // program's sourcemapped shared output with its own — the same clobber the
    // step order above prevents, reached by the path the step order cannot
    // reach. scripts/build.mjs is now the only thing that emits build/.
    "noEmit": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "outDir": "build",
    "rootDir": "src"
  },
  // src/shared is reached through the renderer's imports, so listing two of its
  // files here only ever said which two the author happened to think of.
  //
  // One extension: the renderer holds no JavaScript, so `allowJs`, `checkJs` and
  // a `**/*.js` glob went with the last file they applied to. `**/*.ts` subsumes
  // the ambient `src/renderer/gw-native.d.ts` that used to be named separately.
  "include": ["src/renderer/**/*.ts"]
}
